            "The rules of research ethics were not deduced by philosophers; each was written after people had been harmed. At the <strong>Nuremberg trials</strong> of 1946–47 the world learned what German doctors had done to concentration-camp prisoners in the name of science — freezing, high-altitude, infection and sterilisation ‘experiments’ on people who could not refuse — and the judges' verdict included ten principles, the <strong>Nuremberg Code</strong> of 1947, whose first sentence is that the voluntary consent of the human subject is absolutely essential. The World Medical Association's <strong>Declaration of Helsinki</strong> of 1964, revised many times since, turned the code into guidance for physicians: research must be based on sound science and prior laboratory and animal work, the protocol must be reviewed by an independent committee, the well-being of the participant must take precedence over the interests of science and society, and the vulnerable need special protection. Yet in the United States, from 1932 to 1972, the <strong>Tuskegee syphilis study</strong> followed 399 poor African-American men with syphilis in Alabama to observe the natural course of the disease, told them they were being treated for ‘bad blood’, withheld penicillin after it became the standard treatment in the 1940s, and ended only when a journalist exposed it; the men, their wives and their children paid with their lives and their health, and the mistrust it left persists. The inquiry that followed produced the <strong>Belmont Report</strong> of 1979, which named the three principles — respect for persons, beneficence and justice — that organise this lesson, and the system of institutional review boards that every research institution now runs. The <strong>Council for International Organizations of Medical Sciences (CIOMS)</strong>, with the World Health Organization, issued guidelines in 1982 and most recently in 2016 to apply these principles to research in low- and middle-income countries, where the dangers are different: foreign sponsors testing a drug on a population that will never afford it; trials whose control group receives less than the best available treatment because ‘that is the local standard’; communities that give data and never receive findings. The 1990s trials of short-course zidovudine to prevent mother-to-child HIV transmission in Africa, with placebo controls at a time when a proven regimen existed in the North, forced that debate into the open, and the CIOMS rule that followed is that the control group must receive an established effective intervention unless there are compelling scientific and ethical reasons not to, and that the sponsor must plan for the population to benefit from what is learned.",
