            "<strong>Respect for persons</strong> (autonomy) means that people take part in research only when they have chosen to, with understanding and without pressure, and that those whose autonomy is diminished — children, the unconscious, people with severe mental illness, prisoners — are protected rather than used because they are convenient. In design it becomes informed consent, the right to withdraw without penalty, privacy and confidentiality (lesson 4.2) and honesty about what the study is: deception is permitted only when the research cannot otherwise be done, the risk is minimal and participants are told afterwards. <strong>Beneficence</strong> and <strong>non-maleficence</strong> together require that the study's foreseeable risks — physical (a blood sample, an untested dressing), psychological (an interview about a stillbirth), social (being seen entering the HIV clinic; a quotation that identifies the only male midwife), economic (a day of trading lost) and legal — be identified, minimised and justified by the benefits to participants or to the knowledge that will help people like them; that the study be scientifically sound, because a badly designed study exposes people to risk for an answer nobody can use; that the researcher be competent to do what she proposes; and that harm, when it appears, be acted upon — the trial stopped early by its data safety monitoring board when one arm is clearly better or clearly dangerous, the participant found to have a haemoglobin of 5 g/dL referred that day. <strong>Justice</strong> requires that the burdens and benefits of research be fairly distributed: participants are chosen because the question concerns them, not because they are poor, available or unable to refuse (prisoners, students, one's own staff, the patients on one's own ward); a group that bears the risk of a trial should be among those who benefit from its result; and no one is excluded from the chance to participate — women, the elderly, the illiterate — without a scientific reason. Justice is also why a sponsor from abroad must leave something behind — capacity, equipment, the intervention itself — and why Ghana's committees ask what the community will gain.",
