            "Every study involving people, their tissue or their identifiable records must be reviewed and approved by an independent ethics committee before the first participant is approached. In Ghana the main bodies are the <strong>Ghana Health Service Ethics Review Committee</strong>, which reviews studies conducted in Ghana Health Service facilities and communities; the institutional review boards of the <strong>Noguchi Memorial Institute for Medical Research</strong>, the <strong>University of Ghana</strong> College of Health Sciences, the <strong>Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Technology</strong> Committee on Human Research, Publications and Ethics and the other universities; the committees of the teaching hospitals — Korle Bu, Komfo Anokye, Cape Coast, Tamale, Ho — and of the research centres at Navrongo, Kintampo and Dodowa; and, for trials of medicines and devices, the <strong>Food and Drugs Authority</strong>, whose authorisation is required in addition to ethics approval under the Public Health Act 851. A student submits through her institution's committee and, if the study will be done in a Ghana Health Service facility, obtains the Service's approval as well; a study across several institutions may need several approvals, and the clearance letters, with their numbers and expiry dates, are quoted in the report. Committees include scientists, clinicians, a lawyer or ethicist and lay members from the community, so that the protocol is read by someone who thinks like the participant.",
            "A <strong>submission</strong> contains the full protocol (background, objectives, design, sampling, instruments, analysis plan, timeline, budget); the participant information sheet and consent form in English and in the languages of the participants, with the back-translation; the instruments; the researcher's curriculum vitae and evidence of training in research ethics; letters of permission or support from the facilities and the district; a statement of how data will be stored, who will see them and when they will be destroyed; the plan for managing adverse events and incidental findings; and any conflict of interest and the source of funding. The committee may approve, approve with conditions, require revision or reject; it assesses the science as well as the ethics, because unsound science is unethical. After approval the researcher's duties continue: she conducts the study exactly as approved; submits any <strong>amendment</strong> — a new site, a changed questionnaire, a larger sample — before implementing it; reports serious adverse events and protocol deviations promptly; renews approval annually for a study that lasts more than a year; submits a final report; and keeps the records for inspection. A study begun before approval cannot be approved retrospectively, its data cannot be used, and in a university it is grounds for failing the degree; a Ghana Health Service facility that allows data collection without a clearance letter has itself breached policy, which is why the medical superintendent will ask to see it.",
