            "The participant who tells a researcher that she sells sex in Takoradi, that she hides her antiretrovirals from her husband, that she gave a wrong dose and did not report it, or simply that she is dissatisfied with her matron, has handed over something that can harm her, and the researcher's first duty after consent is to make sure it never does. <strong>Anonymity</strong> means that no one, not even the researcher, can connect a response to a person — a questionnaire with no name, no identification number linked to a list, dropped into a box among a hundred others. <strong>Confidentiality</strong> means that the researcher knows, or could know, who said what, and undertakes not to reveal it: the interview that was recorded, the cohort that must be followed up, the folder review that recorded the hospital number. A study promises only what it can deliver — a focus group is not anonymous and the researcher cannot guarantee that the other members will keep confidence, so the information sheet says so; an interview study is confidential, not anonymous, and says that too. Confidentiality is protected by design: the <strong>identification number</strong> on every form and the key linking numbers to names kept in a separate locked place or an encrypted file; names, hospital numbers, phone numbers and addresses collected only when follow-up requires them and destroyed as soon as it ends; recordings transferred from the phone and deleted; transcripts pseudonymised; paper locked, electronic files password-protected and encrypted, access limited to the named research team; and the whole data set kept for the period the committee specifies and then destroyed. Removing the name is not enough: a report that quotes ‘a 42-year-old male midwife in the district’ has named him if there is only one, and a table that cross-tabulates ward by cadre by HIV status has identified the single pharmacist on the paediatric ward. In <strong>small samples and small communities</strong> the researcher aggregates categories, removes or alters identifying detail that does not change the meaning, and sometimes withholds a quotation altogether; the test is whether the person could be worked out by someone who knows the setting, not whether the name appears.",
