          heading: "Research misconduct and the practices around it",
          paragraphs: [
            "<strong>Research misconduct</strong> is defined almost everywhere by three words. <strong>Fabrication</strong> is making up data or results — the student who, short of time and twenty questionnaires, completes them herself in the hostel; the data collector paid per form who fills a stack without visiting the houses. <strong>Falsification</strong> is manipulating materials, procedures or data so that the research is not accurately represented — changing a value, dropping the participants who spoil the result, moving the blood pressure readings into the range expected, cropping the image. <strong>Plagiarism</strong>, defined in lesson 1.2, is taking another's words, ideas or data without credit — including the student who buys a project, the lecturer who publishes a student's thesis as her own, and the author who republishes her own paper as new. Around the three lie the <strong>questionable research practices</strong> that are not always called misconduct but corrupt the literature as surely: <strong>selective reporting</strong> of the outcomes that worked; <strong>HARKing</strong> — hypothesising after the results are known, and presenting the finding as predicted; <strong>p-hacking</strong> — running test after test and reporting the one that crossed 0.05 (lesson 6.1); <strong>salami slicing</strong> one study into several thin papers; <strong>duplicate publication</strong>; and conducting a study without ethics approval, or departing from the approved protocol in silence. The Ghanaian universities and the Ghana Health Service have research integrity policies, students sign declarations of originality, and the consequences range from a failed degree and a withdrawn paper to dismissal and, for the registered nurse, a finding of professional misconduct by the Nursing and Midwifery Council.",
            "The nurse who sees misconduct — the colleague fabricating forms, the supervisor removing data points, the lecturer demanding authorship — has a duty to act and a fear of acting. The order is the one lesson 4.1's accountability diagram gives for an unsafe instruction: raise it with the person concerned where it is safe to do so; if that fails, report to the principal investigator, the head of department or the research integrity officer; if the person concerned <em>is</em> the principal investigator, report to the ethics committee that approved the study, which has the power to suspend it; and record what was seen, when and to whom it was reported. The <strong>Whistleblower Act, 2006 (Act 720)</strong> protects a person who discloses impropriety in good faith from victimisation; the Ghana Health Service and the universities have their own channels; and a disclosure made honestly is protected even if, after investigation, no misconduct is found. ‘I was told to’ did not protect the nurse before the Council in clinical care, and ‘my supervisor did it’ will not protect the co-author who signed the paper.",
          ],
