            "An <strong>author</strong> is accountable for the work, and the <strong>International Committee of Medical Journal Editors</strong> sets four criteria that must all be met: substantial contribution to the conception or design of the work, or to the acquisition, analysis or interpretation of data; drafting the work or revising it critically for important intellectual content; final approval of the version to be published; and agreement to be accountable for all aspects of the work. Those who contributed without meeting all four — the matron who gave access, the statistician who ran one test, the data collectors, the colleague who proof-read — are named in the <strong>acknowledgements</strong> with their permission, not on the author line. <strong>Gift (honorary) authorship</strong> adds a head of department, a friend or a sponsor who did none of these; <strong>ghost authorship</strong> omits someone who did — the student whose thesis appears under the supervisor's name alone, or the company writer who drafted the paper; <strong>guest authorship</strong> borrows a famous name. The order of authors is agreed at the start of the project, not fought over at the end; the first author did most of the work and the last is usually the senior researcher who supervised it, and the student is normally the first author of a paper from her own thesis.",
            "<strong>Predatory journals</strong> have multiplied around African researchers under pressure to publish: they email invitations, promise review in days and publication in weeks, charge a fee of a few hundred dollars, have no real peer review, and vanish with the paper and the money. The signs are the unsolicited flattering email, a title that imitates a real journal, an impact factor from an agency nobody has heard of, an editorial board of people who did not agree to serve, a scope that covers every discipline, and an address that does not exist; the checks are whether the journal is indexed in MEDLINE/PubMed, Scopus or the Directory of Open Access Journals, whether its publisher belongs to the Committee on Publication Ethics, and the ‘Think. Check. Submit.’ checklist. A paper in a predatory journal is lost to the literature, counts for nothing in a proper promotion review, and cannot be withdrawn and submitted elsewhere because it is technically published. <strong>Conflicts of interest</strong> — the dressing trial funded by the dressing's manufacturer, the author who owns shares, the evaluator studying her own programme, the sponsor who wants the right to veto publication — do not disqualify a study but must be declared on the ethics form, in the paper and at the conference, so that the reader can weigh them; the undeclared conflict discovered later destroys the credibility of the work however sound it was.",
